{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true
  },
  /* **`vite.config.ts` alone — the one beside this file — and nothing the root program includes
     may join it, least of all `packages/ui/lib/iconFont.ts`, which that config reaches through
     the `vite.base.ts` it is merged over.**

     `composite: true` makes every file listed here an *output* of a referenced project, and
     the root `tsconfig.json` includes `packages/ui`. List a file in both and `tsc` demands the
     built declaration beside it: `error TS6305: Output file 'src/lib/iconFont.d.ts' has not been
     built from source file 'src/lib/iconFont.ts'` — with `include pattern 'src'` named as the
     reason. (The error as it was printed, when the shared UI was `src/` and this file was at
     the repository root.) Nothing ever builds this project (see below), so that `.d.ts` cannot
     exist and `npm run build` fails on a clean checkout. It shipped that way in `798e25e` and
     took `main`'s CI red on two consecutive pushes.

     The import is checked by nothing today and that is the honest reading of this file:
     `npm run build` runs `tsc` and a `tsc -p` for each of the other programs — `package.json`'s
     `build` line is the list — and this project is not on it, so no script compiles it at all
     and the reference in the root `tsconfig.json` is a declared relationship rather than a
     check. Vite type-strips its own config. If `vite.config.ts` is ever to be genuinely
     type-checked, the way in is a build step of its own — not a wider `include` here, which is
     the thing that breaks. */
  "include": ["vite.config.ts"]
}
